  "summary_text": "Kyle, Saturday was a study in capacity constraints masking fundamentally sound underlying performance.\n\nPort Washington ran four groomers and delivered $396 per groomer-day\u201421% below the trailing 30-day baseline of $503. This is a productivity miss, not a calendar issue. The DOW-adjusted expectation of $55,779 MTD versus your actual $55,091 shows you're only 1.2% behind where you should be on a normalized basis, which is clean. But yesterday's transaction count collapsed 30% while core grooming revenue fell 32%\u2014this points to scheduling or availability, not demand. The bright spot: retail jumped 45% and add-on attach improved to 75%, signaling strong front-of-house execution and upsell discipline. The visit-cadence drift is shrinking (15.5% to 13.3%), a leading indicator that retention is improving.\n\nHicksville is the real concern. One groomer yesterday produced only $430\u2014basically at baseline\u2014but the week-to-date revenue miss of 36% and appointment miss of 35% cannot be explained by one light Saturday. You're scheduled for zero groomers today and one tomorrow, with only 13 groomer-days over the next seven days. That's understaffing, not demand weakness. MTD you're actually up 5.8% on a DOW-adjusted basis, so the store can perform, but the visit-cadence drift is growing (10.2% to 12.6%)\u2014stealth churn warning. Add-on revenue collapsed to 33% attach (from 50% last week) and retail dropped 57%, suggesting reduced transaction volume per active groomer is straining the upsell and front-of-house function.\n\nBoth locations have critical inventory gaps (X-Small Treat Table at zero stock on both stores). Port Washington's retention story is improving; Hicksville's is deteriorating. The constraint is capacity, not capability.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington has 0 groomers scheduled today (7.0h bathers) and 5 tomorrow (7.0h bathers); Hicksville has 0 today and 1 tomorrow; next 7 days Port Washington runs 34 groomer-days and Hicksville runs 13 groomer-days.**"
